     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-20 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Parental Wealth Nearly Doubles Housing Gap for Young Norwegians</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Housing</span> <span>#Wealth Gap</span> <span>#Norway</span> <span>#Intergenerational Wealth</span></div>
       <p>The disparity in homeownership among 30-year-olds in Norway, based on their parents' wealth, has almost doubled over a decade, according to a study published by Phys.org. Researchers found that in 2005, the difference in homeownership between young adults with parents in the wealthiest fifth of the population and those with parents in the least wealthy fifth was approximately 10 percentage points. By 2017, this gap had expanded to around 18 percentage points.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-20/parental-wealth-nearly-doubles-housing-gap-for-young-norwegi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The study, titled &quot;The housing channel of intergenerational wealth persistence,&quot; was conducted by Ragnar Juelsrud, an associate professor at the University of Oslo's Department of Economics, alongside Ella Getz Wold, Knut Are Aastveit, Eirik Eylands Brandsaas, and Gisle James Natvik. Utilizing registry data from Statistics Norway and the Norwegian Mapping Authority, the team tracked parents and children across the entire Norwegian population from 1992 to 2017. Their findings indicate that nearly seven out of ten 30-year-olds with wealthy parents own a home, compared to about four out of ten with less wealthy parents.</p><p>While factors such as young people's own income, education, and place of residence explain about half of this gap, the remaining half is directly linked to parents' financial wealth, which includes bank deposits, shares, and mutual funds. Juelsrud highlights that &quot;Financial support from parents is an important ticket into the housing market.&quot; A 2023 online survey of 240 parents over 40 revealed that while eight out of ten parents wished to help their children with housing, nearly nine out of ten wealthy parents had helped or would, versus fewer than four out of ten less wealthy parents, underscoring a difference in ability rather than willingness.</p><p>Parents assist their children in four primary ways, all more common among wealthier families: transferring money from savings, taking additional loans against their own homes, co-buying properties, or selling homes to children often at more than 25% below market value—a discount averaging NOK 740,000 in 2015 prices, or about NOK 1 million today. These methods explain over half of the wealth-related difference, with borrowing against their own home being the most significant channel. Early homeownership, largely debt-financed, generates substantial returns on equity through price growth, creating a lasting wealth advantage that can persist into a child's early 40s, even if those with less affluent parents eventually catch up in homeownership.</p><p>Public statistics from Statistics Norway's 2023 living conditions survey show that approximately one in four households purchasing a home in the previous four years received parental assistance, a proportion stable since 2012. Among homebuyers under 30, nearly half received parental support, according to a 2015 survey. These figures reinforce the study's findings on the critical role of intergenerational financial support in housing market entry.</p><p>What to watch: The long-term implications for intergenerational wealth accumulation and social mobility.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of the study's methodology, findings, and the economic channels identified. It incorporates the statistics and expert quotes provided in the source material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-housing-gap-young-people-wealthy.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
