     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-10-20 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Suspect Arrested After Limoges City Hall Fire Amid Widespread French Protests</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#France</span> <span>#protests</span> <span>#Limoges</span> <span>#curfew</span></div>
       <p>A 19th-century city hall in Limoges was gutted by fire on Saturday during mass disorder, with its clock tower and parts of the roof dramatically collapsing. French media reports indicate a 34-year-old suspect, linked to a far-left movement and known to police for violence against public officials, has been arrested. The suspect is on France's national security watchlist, known as the &quot;S&quot; list, according to FranceInfo and BFMTV.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-20/suspect-arrested-after-limoges-city-hall-fire-amid-widesprea.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The fire in Limoges occurred amidst widespread demonstrations across France, which began last month and escalated over the weekend. Hundreds of thousands of demonstrators took to the streets to protest rising fuel prices, the cost of living, and deteriorating public services, including schools. Officials reported more than 2,500 arrests since the disorder broke out, with French Justice Minister Gérald Darmanin telling CNews-Europe that over 120 people have already been imprisoned. About 1,000 police officers were injured, and one demonstrator's hand was blown off by a crowd-control grenade, according to AFP.</p><p>More than 2,000 shops and other businesses were directly impacted by the weekend's violence, as rioters set fire to cars and buildings and looted businesses in Paris, Marseille, and other cities. In response, targeted curfews were introduced across France on Monday, contributing to a quieter night. Marseille's mayor, Benoît Payan, announced a curfew for young people in commercial areas starting Tuesday evening. Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu thanked police for enforcing these measures, while Justice Minister Darmanin requested prosecutors be &quot;extremely firm&quot; and &quot;ruthless&quot; against those who attacked public officials and looted shops, though he insisted a state of emergency was not needed.</p><p>Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez admitted police had &quot;lost control&quot; in some cities and offered his resignation on Monday, which was rejected by President Emmanuel Macron. Looking ahead, protest organisers, including the &quot;yellow vests&quot; (gilets jaunes), have declared a &quot;Act II&quot; of protests for this upcoming Saturday, and the French union La CGT has called for a major day of strikes on 5 November.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Thorough reporting covering the Limoges city hall fire, the suspect, curfews, arrests, and ministerial reactions accurately.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cw5yn5e8mezvo?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
